#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707413 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}} r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, holding $\\theta$ constant. Substitute both radial limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $u=\\sin\\theta$, so $\\mathrm d u=\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta$.\n\nEvaluate at the upper angular limit and subtract the lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(\\frac{2 \\sin^{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707415 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int_{\\csc{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{2} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, holding $\\theta$ constant. Substitute both radial limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 1 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{\\csc{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{2}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 1\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}-\\left(\\csc{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 - \\frac{\\csc^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at the upper angular limit and subtract the lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\left[2 \\theta + \\frac{\\cot{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{\\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}}{2} + \\pi\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(\\frac{\\cot{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\pi\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707424 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int_{1 - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{1} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, holding $\\theta$ constant. Substitute both radial limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{1 - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}^{1}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}-\\left(1 - \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the identity\n$$\n\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right).\n$$\n\nThen the angular integral is\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIntegrate each angular term, including its frequency factor.\n\nEvaluate at the upper angular limit and subtract the lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\pi \\right)}}{4} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}\\right) \\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{8} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right)}}{4} + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\left(2 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{8} - \\frac{1}{4} + \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\sqrt{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{8} + \\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707442 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+1}\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nRemember: both component functions must be defined, and a real square root allows a zero radicand." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, where\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+1},\\qquad Q(x,y)=x^2+y.\n$$\n\nThe square root is real exactly when\n\n$$\ny+1\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge -1.\n$$\n\nThus $D_P=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid y\\ge -1\\}$. The polynomial $Q$ is defined on all of $\\mathbb R^2$, so $D_Q=\\mathbb R^2$. The domain of the vector field is the intersection:\n\n$$\nD=D_P\\cap D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid y\\ge -1\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the half-plane above $y=-1$, including the boundary line. There is no restriction on $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707449 :question/problem "Which shaded region depicts the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y+x}}\\,\\mathbf i+\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}\\,\\mathbf j\\,?\n$$\n\nSolid boundaries are included. Dashed boundaries and open circles are excluded." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, where\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y+x}},\\qquad Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}.\n$$\n\nFor $P$, the square root must be real and its value must be nonzero because it is in the denominator. Thus\n\n$$\ny+x>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y>-x.\n$$\n\nSo $D_P=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid y>-x\\}$ is the half-plane above the excluded line $y=-x$.\n\n![](images/40/405d25b4a3d92ce89584a26354a5fd7e55af0ae90d70d865fff1b7575b09c00e.png)\n\nFor $Q$, a zero radicand is allowed:\n\n$$\n4-x^2-y^2\\ge0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2\\le4.\n$$\n\nSo $D_Q=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x^2+y^2\\le4\\}$ is the disk of radius $2$, including its circle.\n\n![](images/0e/0ec8bb2a7ff212ffc5928984cd6ed38ee36fc0bde69e1d4d5604a2e1d6bf43ca.png)\n\nBoth conditions must hold. Their intersection is\n\n$$\nD=D_P\\cap D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid y>-x,\\ x^2+y^2\\le4\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe domain is the half-disk above $y=-x$. Its circular arc is included except for the two endpoints $(\\sqrt2,-\\sqrt2)$ and $(-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2)$, which lie on the excluded line. The diagonal diameter, including the origin, is excluded.\n\n![](images/ec/ec7c745c4cf1e295fdfe4d0e5b9b5b47c9b00e2fb5421699d6655acc6d5d169b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707451 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{x}{x^2-4y^2}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, where\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},\\qquad Q(x,y)=\\frac{x}{x^2-4y^2}.\n$$\n\nEach denominator must be nonzero. For $P$,\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad (x,y)=(0,0),\n$$\n\nso $D_P=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid(x,y)\\ne(0,0)\\}$. For $Q$, factor the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y).\n$$\n\nIt vanishes on either line $x=2y$ or $x=-2y$. Hence\n\n$$\nD_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x\\ne 2y,\\ x\\ne -2y\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe origin already lies on both excluded lines, so the restriction from $P$ removes no further points. Therefore\n\n$$\nD=D_P\\cap D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x\\ne 2y,\\ x\\ne -2y\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nBoth entire lines are excluded, even at points where a numerator is zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707459 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $x=1$. Given that $P(1,2,3)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $y$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $x=1$ holds $x$ constant, so $y$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dy=f_y(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_y(1,2)&=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(2)^{2}}+(2)\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{4}+2(1) \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $3$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707461 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=2$. Given that $P(0,2,-7)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)={4}^{x+y}y(x\\ln (4)+1).\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=2$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(0,2)&={4}^{0+2}(2)(0\\cdot \\ln (4)+1)=32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $-7$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707463 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=1$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=y e^{\\sin(xy)}\\cos(xy).\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=1$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(\\pi,1)&=(1)e^{\\sin((\\pi)(1))}\\cos((\\pi)(1)) \\\\\n&=(1)e^{0}(-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707464 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=\\pi$. Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=y\\cos x + \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=\\pi$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\pi)&=(\\pi)\\cos \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\cos (\\pi) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707467 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is its intersection with the plane $y=3$. Given that $P(-3,3,2)$ lies on $C$ and that\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFind the exact slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$, measured with respect to $x$. $m=$ {{slope}}" :question/worked-solution "The plane $y=3$ holds $y$ constant, so $x$ varies and the section slope is $dz/dx=f_x(x,y)$. Substitute the ordered coordinates of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f_x(-3,3)&=5(-3)^{2}(3)=135\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe height $2$ is not an input to the supplied partial derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707478 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $G(x,y)=\\ln(x+2)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+2>0\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y+1},\\ln(x+y+3)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y+1\\geq0,\\ x+y+3\\geq0\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $V(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{y+1}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+2\\geq0,\\ y+1\\geq0\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $G$ The components require $x+2>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x+2)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x+2),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $F$ The components require $x-y+1\\geq0,\\ x+y+3>0$. The point $(-2,-1)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\ln(0)$; the logarithm is undefined. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **III.** $V$ The components require $x+2\\ne0,\\ y+1\\geq0$. The point $(-2,0)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $1/0$; the reciprocal is undefined. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\nSelect I only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707494 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $K(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y-1},\\ln(x+y-1)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y-1\\geq-1,\\ x+y-1>0\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $E(x,y)=\\ln(x-1)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-1\\geq1\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $B(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x-1},\\sqrt{y}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-1\\ne0,\\ y\\geq0\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $K$ The components require $x-y-1\\geq0,\\ x+y-1>0$. The point $(1,1)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\sqrt{-1}$; the square root is not real. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **II.** $E$ The components require $x-1>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x-1)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x-1),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **III.** $B$ The components require $x-1\\ne0,\\ y\\geq0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The square-root boundary $y=0$ is allowed when $x-1\\ne0$.  The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\nSelect II, III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707495 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $B(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x-2},\\sqrt{y-3}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-2\\ne0,\\ y-3\\geq-1\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $M(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y+1},\\ln(x+y-5)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y+1\\geq-1,\\ x+y-5>0\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $J(x,y)=\\ln(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-2\\geq1\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $B$ The components require $x-2\\ne0,\\ y-3\\geq0$. The point $(3,2)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\sqrt{-1}$; the square root is not real. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **II.** $M$ The components require $x-y+1\\geq0,\\ x+y-5>0$. The point $(2,4)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\sqrt{-1}$; the square root is not real. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **III.** $J$ The components require $x-2>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x-2),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\nSelect III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707511 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $D(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x-2},\\sqrt{y-2}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-2\\ne0,\\ y-2\\geq0\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=\\ln(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-2\\geq0\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $N(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y},\\ln(x+y-4)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y\\geq0,\\ x+y-4>0\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $D$ The components require $x-2\\ne0,\\ y-2\\geq0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The square-root boundary $y-2=0$ is allowed when $x-2\\ne0$.  The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $F$ The components require $x-2>0$. The point $(2,2)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $\\ln(0)$; the logarithm is undefined. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x-2)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x-2),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\n- **III.** $N$ The components require $x-y\\geq0,\\ x+y-4>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The boundary $x-2=y-2>0$ is allowed. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\nSelect I, III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707518 :question/problem "Which formulas define vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$ on their **entire stated domains**? Select all that apply. Keep every stated domain unchanged. The symbols $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ are the planar unit vectors.\n\n- **I.** $T(x,y)=\\langle\\sqrt{x-y+6},\\ln(x+y)\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x-y+6\\geq0,\\ x+y>0\\}$.\n\n- **II.** $M(x,y)=\\ln(x+3)\\,\\mathbf i$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+3>0\\}$.\n\n- **III.** $J(x,y)=\\langle\\frac{1}{x+3},\\sqrt{y-3}\\rangle$ on $\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x+3\\geq0,\\ y-3\\geq0\\}$." :question/worked-solution "Check planar inputs and outputs, then test whether the formula is real and defined at every point of the stated domain.\n\n- **I.** $T$ The components require $x-y+6\\geq0,\\ x+y>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The boundary $x+3=y-3>0$ is allowed. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $M$ The components require $x+3>0$. Every point in the stated domain satisfies these conditions. The omitted planar component is zero: $\\ln(x+3)\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j=\\langle\\ln(x+3),0\\rangle$. Its output is still planar. The inputs and outputs are planar, so select this function.\n\n- **III.** $J$ The components require $x+3\\ne0,\\ y-3\\geq0$. The point $(-3,4)$ lies in the stated domain, but gives $1/0$; the reciprocal is undefined. The formula therefore fails on the entire stated domain. Do not select this function; shrinking the domain is not allowed in this question.\n\nSelect I, II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707533 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply. Use each function's declared input coordinates and its natural real domain.\n\n- **I.** $Z(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{(x+2)})\\,\\mathbf i+(0)\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- **II.** $S(x,y)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)}$\n\n- **III.** $A(x,y)=\\langle (x+2)^2,(x+2)^2\\rangle$" :question/worked-solution "Check the input dimension and output dimension.\n\n- **I.** $Z$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x+2)\\ne 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $S$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a scalar. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x+2)\\ne 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\n- **III.** $A$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. All components are real on $\\mathbb R^2$. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\nSelect I, III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707551 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply. Use each function's declared input coordinates and its natural real domain.\n\n- **I.** $S(x,y)=\\langle \\ln (x+1),0\\rangle$\n\n- **II.** $E(x,y)=\\langle \\ln (x+1),\\ln (x+1),1\\rangle$\n\n- **III.** $C(x,y,z)=(\\sin (x+1))\\,\\mathbf i+(1)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Check the input dimension and output dimension.\n\n- **I.** $S$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x+1)>0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $E$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 3 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x+1)>0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\n- **III.** $C$ takes 3 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. All components are real on $\\mathbb R^3$. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\nSelect I only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707562 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply. Use each function's declared input coordinates and its natural real domain.\n\n- **I.** $D(t)=\\langle \\sqrt{(t-2)},\\sqrt{(t-2)}\\rangle$\n\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=(\\ln (x-2))\\,\\mathbf i+(0)\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- **III.** $T(x,y)=(\\ln (x-2))\\,\\mathbf i+(0)\\,\\mathbf j+(2)\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Check the input dimension and output dimension.\n\n- **I.** $D$ takes 1 input coordinate and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(t)\\in \\mathbb R^1:(t-2)\\geq 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\n- **II.** $F$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x-2)>0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **III.** $T$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 3 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:(x-2)>0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\nSelect II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707564 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply. Use each function's declared input coordinates and its natural real domain.\n\n- **I.** $W(t)=\\langle t^2,0\\rangle$\n\n- **II.** $D(x,y)=(\\sqrt{x})\\,\\mathbf i+(1)\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=\\langle \\sin x,\\sin x\\rangle$" :question/worked-solution "Check the input dimension and output dimension.\n\n- **I.** $W$ takes 1 input coordinate and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. All components are real on $\\mathbb R^1$. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\n- **II.** $D$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:x\\geq 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **III.** $F$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. The declared input list determines the input dimension, even when a coordinate is unused; a zero output coordinate still counts. All components are real on $\\mathbb R^2$. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\nSelect II, III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707661 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2$. The point $P(5,-2,-4)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=-\\left(y\\sqrt{\\left(x - 1\\right)}\\right)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so $x$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_x(5,-2) \\\\ &=-\\left(\\left(-2\\right)\\sqrt{\\left(\\left(5\\right) - 1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(x_0,z_0)=(5,-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(-4)&=(4)(x-(5)) \\\\ z&=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=4 x - 24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707666 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2$. The point $P(2,-1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4},3)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=-\\left(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\left(y + 1\\right))\\right)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $x$, so $y$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_y(2,-1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\\\ &=-\\left(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(-1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1\\right))\\right) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(y_0,z_0)=(-1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4},3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(3)&=(-4)(y-(-1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4})) \\\\ z&=- 4 y - 1 + \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=- 4 y - 1 + \\pi\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707672 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2$. The point $P(2,1,3)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=-\\left(x{e}^{x\\left(y - 1\\right)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(y - 1\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $x$, so $y$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_y(2,1) \\\\ &=-\\left(\\left(2\\right){e}^{\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(1\\right) - 1\\right)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\left(1\\right) - 1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(y_0,z_0)=(1,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(3)&=(0)(y-(1)) \\\\ z&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707682 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1$. The point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so $x$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_x(1,1) \\\\ &=3\\left(1\\right)+6\\left(1\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\left(1\\right)\\left(1\\right)) \\\\ &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(x_0,z_0)=(1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(1)&=(3)(x-(1)) \\\\ z&=3 x - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3 x - 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707684 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2$. The point $P(3,-2,1)$ lies on $C$, and\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=-\\left({y}^{2}\\left(x - 1\\right)+2y-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nFind the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "The plane fixes $y$, so $x$ varies along the section. Evaluate the supplied partial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m&=f_x(3,-2) \\\\ &=-\\left({\\left(-2\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\left(3\\right) - 1\\right)+2\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(\\left(3\\right) - 1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form at $(x_0,z_0)=(3,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-(1)&=(-2)(x-(3)) \\\\ z&=7 - 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent retains the fixed-coordinate plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=7 - 2 x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707696 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Check both the input points and the output vectors.\n\n- $A$ takes a point $(x,y,z)$ and returns the three-component vector $\\langle x+y,z,x\\rangle$. Its components are defined for all real $x,y,z$, so it is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- $B$ takes a point $(x,y)$ in $\\mathbb R^2$ and returns a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. It is not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- $C$ returns a vector with three components, but its input is a single real parameter $t$. It describes a vector-valued function of one variable, not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $A$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707699 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ must take three-dimensional points as inputs and return vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$. A restricted domain is allowed.\n\n- $D$ takes $(x,y,z)$ and returns a column vector with three components. The denominator requires $x\\ne-1$, so its domain is\n$$\nS_D=\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\mid x\\ne-1\\}.\n$$\nIt is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- $E$ returns three components, but takes only $(x,y)$ as input. Its domain is $\\mathbb R^2$, so it is not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ as given. A three-component output does not turn a two-coordinate input into a three-coordinate input.\n- $F$ takes three coordinates but returns the scalar $xy+z^2$, not a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. It is a scalar-valued function, not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $D$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707714 :question/problem "Here $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,\\mathbf k$ are the standard unit vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$. Use each function's natural real domain.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All three functions take points $(x,y,z)$ as inputs. Now determine what kind of output each expression actually represents.\n\n- $G$ is written using the standard unit vectors of $\\mathbb R^3$. The missing $\\mathbf k$ term has coefficient zero, so\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=\\langle\\ln z,\\sqrt{x-y},0\\rangle.\n$$\nIts domain is\n$$\nS_G=\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\mid z>0,\\ x\\ge y\\}.\n$$\nThus $G$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of $S_G$. Its outputs lying in the $xy$-plane does not prevent it from being a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- $H$ explicitly returns an ordered pair, $\\langle\\ln z,\\sqrt{x-y}\\rangle$, in $\\mathbb R^2$. It has the same domain restrictions as $G$, but it is not a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ as written. An ordered pair does not acquire an extra coordinate automatically.\n- $K$ explicitly returns an ordered triple. Its domain is\n$$\nS_K=\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\mid x\\ne y\\}.\n$$\nFor each point in this set, $K$ returns $\\langle0,1/(x-y),0\\rangle\\in\\mathbb R^3$. Zero components and independence from $z$ do not change its three-coordinate input or its three-component output. It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $G$ and $K$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707763 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=5$. The point $P(-4,5,3)$ lies on $C$. The partial derivative is supplied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Holding $y$ fixed means that $x$ varies, so the slice slope is $f_x(-4,5)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_x(-4,5) & =-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\left(5\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(-4\\right)}^{2}}}{2\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(5\\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{3}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed quotient is evaluated at the supplied point; its denominator is nonzero. Simplifying the numerator and denominator gives the exact slope above.\n\nA change of $1$ in $x$ along the tangent gives a change of $\\frac{3}{13}$ in $z$, with no change in $y$. A tangent direction is $\\langle 1,0,\\frac{3}{13}\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(-4,5,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y&=5 \\\\ z-(3)&=\\left(\\frac{3}{13}\\right)(x-(-4))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane is equivalently $\\frac{3 x}{13} - z + \\frac{51}{13}=0$. Normal vectors are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 0,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle - \\frac{3}{13},0,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ - \\frac{3}{13} & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =\\langle 1,0,\\frac{3}{13}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple is valid. The listed answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\left(\\langle 1,0,\\frac{3}{13}\\rangle\\right) & =\\langle -3,0,- \\frac{9}{13}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt has the required component ratio $v_z/v_x=\\frac{3}{13}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707768 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-1$. The point $P(1,-1,-3)$ lies on $C$. The partial derivative is supplied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =-\\left(3{e}^{x+y}-\\ln |xy|\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Holding $y$ fixed means that $x$ varies, so the slice slope is $f_x(1,-1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_x(1,-1) & =-\\left(3{e}^{\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)}-\\ln |\\left(1\\right)\\left(-1\\right)|\\right) \\\\ & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA change of $1$ in $x$ along the tangent gives a change of $-3$ in $z$, with no change in $y$. A tangent direction is $\\langle 1,0,-3\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(1,-1,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y&=-1 \\\\ z-(-3)&=(-3)(x-(1))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane is equivalently $- 3 x - z=0$. Normal vectors are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 0,1,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 3,0,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =\\langle 1,0,-3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple is valid. The listed answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\left(\\langle 1,0,-3\\rangle\\right) & =\\langle -3,0,9\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt has the required component ratio $v_z/v_x=-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707770 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0$. The point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $C$. The partial derivative is supplied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_y(x,y) & =-\\left(2(y+x)-2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Holding $x$ fixed means that $y$ varies, so the slice slope is $f_y(0,0)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_y(0,0) & =-\\left(2(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right))-2\\right) \\\\ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA change of $1$ in $y$ along the tangent gives a change of $2$ in $z$, with no change in $x$. A tangent direction is $\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(0,0,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=0 \\\\ z-(-2)&=(2)(y-(0))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane is equivalently $2 y - z - 2=0$. Normal vectors are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,-2,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =\\langle 0,-1,-2\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNegating this cross product gives $\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$.\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple is valid. The listed answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\left(\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle\\right) & =\\langle 0,-3,-6\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt has the required component ratio $v_z/v_y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707790 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2$. The point $P(-2,1,-2)$ lies on $C$. The partial derivative is supplied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_y(x,y) & =\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Holding $x$ fixed means that $y$ varies, so the slice slope is $f_y(-2,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_y(-2,1) & =\\frac{\\left(1\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(-2\\right)(\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right))}} \\\\ & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed quotient is evaluated at the supplied point; its denominator is nonzero. Simplifying the numerator and denominator gives the exact slope above.\n\nA change of $1$ in $y$ along the tangent gives a change of $\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$ in $z$, with no change in $x$. A tangent direction is $\\langle 0,1,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(-2,1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=-2 \\\\ z-(-2)&=\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(y-(1))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane is equivalently $\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} y}{2} - z - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2=0$. Normal vectors are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =\\langle 0,-1,- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNegating this cross product gives $\\langle 0,1,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle$.\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple is valid. The listed answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\left(\\langle 0,1,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\rangle\\right) & =\\langle 0,-2,- 3 \\sqrt{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt has the required component ratio $v_z/v_y=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707793 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2$. The point $P(2,-1,2)$ lies on $C$. The partial derivative is supplied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_y(x,y) & ={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-\\ln (y+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Holding $x$ fixed means that $y$ varies, so the slice slope is $f_y(2,-1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=f_y(2,-1) & ={\\left(-1\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(2\\right)}^{2}-\\ln (\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)) \\\\ & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA change of $1$ in $y$ along the tangent gives a change of $5$ in $z$, with no change in $x$. A tangent direction is $\\langle 0,1,5\\rangle$.\n\nThe tangent line through $P(2,-1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=2 \\\\ z-(2)&=(5)(y-(-1))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe second plane is equivalently $5 y - z + 7=0$. Normal vectors are $\\mathbf n_1=\\langle 1,0,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf n_2=\\langle 0,-5,1\\rangle$. Their cross product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n_1\\times\\mathbf n_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ & =\\langle 0,-1,-5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNegating this cross product gives $\\langle 0,1,5\\rangle$.\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple is valid. The listed answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\left(\\langle 0,1,5\\rangle\\right) & =\\langle 0,-1,-5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt has the required component ratio $v_z/v_y=5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707894 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x y}{x - 2}\\,i+\\left(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2} - 6} \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\frac{x y}{x - 2}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2} - 6} \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x - 2 \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x \\neq 2\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x^{2} + y^{2} - 6 \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 6\\right\\}$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,\\begin{aligned}&x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 6\\\\&x \\neq 2\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707901 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(x + 3 y \\right)}\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right)\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(x + 3 y \\right)}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: The logarithm argument must be strictly positive.\n\nThe required conditions are $x + 3 y > 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x + 3 y > 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nThe logarithm's strict inequality already excludes the origin. The domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x + 3 y > 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707909 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2}}\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right)\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\frac{x}{25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2}}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) y}{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} \\neq 0$.\n\nFactor the denominator: $25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2}=\\left(5 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(5 x + 2 y\\right)$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nThe origin lies on both excluded lines, so removing those lines already removes the origin. The domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,25 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} \\neq 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707924 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2} - 14} \\right)}\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) x y}{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} + y^{2} - 14} \\right)}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right) x y}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x^{2} + y^{2} - 14 \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 14\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $y \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,y \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,\\begin{aligned}&x^{2} + y^{2} \\neq 14\\\\&y \\neq 0\\end{aligned}\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707928 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(- 6 x^{2} + y - 3 \\right)}\\,i+\\left(\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right) e^{\\frac{1}{y}}\\right)\\,j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(- 6 x^{2} + y - 3 \\right)}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right) e^{\\frac{1}{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: The logarithm argument must be strictly positive.\n\nThe required conditions are $- 6 x^{2} + y - 3 > 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,- 6 x^{2} + y - 3 > 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $y \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,y \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nThe logarithm's strict inequality already excludes the zero denominator in the exponential. The domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,- 6 x^{2} + y - 3 > 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707950 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=x^{2}\\,i+\\left(\\ln{\\left(y + 3 \\right)}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nRemember: both component functions must be defined." :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=x^{2}\\\\Q(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(y + 3 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: This polynomial is defined everywhere in the real plane.\n\nThus $D_P=\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nQ: The logarithm argument must be strictly positive.\n\nThe required conditions are $y + 3 > 0$.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,y + 3 > 0\\right\\}$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,y + 3 > 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707955 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(x + 1 \\right)}\\,i+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nRemember: both component functions must be defined." :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(x + 1 \\right)}\\\\Q(x,y)&=y^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: The logarithm argument must be strictly positive.\n\nThe required conditions are $x + 1 > 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x + 1 > 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: This polynomial is defined everywhere in the real plane.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x + 1 > 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
